WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.227
Use this checkpoint to test function choice.

00:00:02.227 --> 00:00:06.992
Boundary spaces suggest trimming. A reliable delimiter suggests SPLIT_PART.

00:00:06.992 --> 00:00:11.862
A full month grouping key suggests DATE_TRUNC rather than only extracting the month number.

00:00:11.862 --> 00:00:14.427
Timestamp subtraction returns an interval.

00:00:14.427 --> 00:00:17.457
TO_CHAR is generally a presentation step.

00:00:17.457 --> 00:00:21.107
The important skill is matching the function to the meaning of the task.

00:00:21.107 --> 00:00:24.727
For this activity, make a prediction before you run anything.

00:00:24.727 --> 00:00:28.972
State what one output row should represent, which records you expect to remain,

00:00:28.972 --> 00:00:31.992
and what edge case is most likely to expose a mistake.

00:00:31.992 --> 00:00:35.487
Then run the SQL and compare the result with that prediction.

00:00:35.487 --> 00:00:41.557
If it does not match, debug the logic in small pieces instead of changing several clauses at once.

00:00:41.557 --> 00:00:47.007
This is the same habit you would use in a real analytics project: validate the meaning of the result,

00:00:47.007 --> 00:00:49.097
not only whether the statement executes.
